    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d640">

                <pb facs="0806" n="696" sameAs="frus:frus1931v03/pg696"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94/3267: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1931-12-21T15:00:00+08:00">December 21, 1931—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 21—7:42 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>I have read your press statement of December 10, especially the final paragraph,
                    to Dr. Wellington Koo.</p>
                <pb facs="0807" n="697" xml:id="pg697"/>
                <p>Today he sends Hsu Mo to me to say that the Chinese Government is extremely
                    concerned over the Chinchow situation in view of statements which emanate every
                    day from Mukden to indicate preparations by the Japanese for an attack on that
                    city. He points out the reenforcement of Japanese troops at Tientsin and the
                    sending of additional troops to Shanhaikwan, thereby enabling the Japanese to
                    cut off any withdrawal by Chinese forces from Chinchow in the event of a
                    Japanese attack. Koo asks whether there is anything my Government can do to
                    forestall a Japanese attack upon Chinchow.</p>
                <p>I have informed Hsu Mo that Koo’s request would be transmitted to the Department.
                    I observed that I could not believe the Japanese would launch an attack upon
                    Chinchow in view of the League Council’s resolution, but he pointed out that in
                    the past the Japanese have sought and found pretexts and now may seek them
                    again. It is stated by Koo that the fear is entertained lest an attack upon
                    Chinchow should disturb very seriously peace and order throughout North
                    China.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
